Don't overwrite a transmit gain that was set before the stream started

patches/0004 mutes the transmitter whenever no DMA buffer is streaming and
unmutes when one starts, restoring the attenuation ad9361_tx_mute() cached
at the previous mute. Restoring unconditionally is the problem: setting a
gain and then starting the stream is the obvious order, and it is the order
most tools use, but the unmute then overwrites that gain a moment later
with a value cached at the end of the PREVIOUS transmission. Measured on
hardware: asking for -10 dB put -60 dB on the wire.

The workarounds people reach for - re-applying the gain after the stream
has started, or a watchdog script polling buffer/enable and writing a gain
of its own - are both symptoms of this, and the second is worse than the
disease: it applies a fixed gain to both channels a second or two after
any stream starts, overriding the application silently. On a board with the
PGA-102+ power amplifier fitted, the 10 dB such scripts commonly use is
roughly +13 dBm at the SMA, against a receive port rated to +2.5 dBm.

So restore the cached gain only when nobody has set one since the mute.
That keeps what is useful about it - start a stream having set nothing, and
your last gain comes back - and drops the surprise. ad9361_tx_is_muted()
is the test, exported alongside ad9361_tx_mute() which it sits next to.

--- a/linux/drivers/iio/adc/ad9361.c
+++ b/linux/drivers/iio/adc/ad9361.c
@@ -1311,6 +1311,20 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(ad9361_tx_mute);
 
+/**
+ * ad9361_tx_is_muted - are both transmit attenuators still at full attenuation?
+ *
+ * Lets a caller tell "nobody has touched the gain since we muted" apart from
+ * "the application has set one of its own since". That is the difference
+ * between restoring a cached gain being helpful and it being destructive.
+ */
+bool ad9361_tx_is_muted(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy)
+{
+	return ad9361_get_tx_atten(phy, 1) == MAX_TX_ATTENUATION_DB &&
+	       ad9361_get_tx_atten(phy, 2) == MAX_TX_ATTENUATION_DB;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(ad9361_tx_is_muted);
+
 static int ad9361_trx_ext_lo_control(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy,
 				     bool tx, bool enable)
 {
--- a/linux/drivers/iio/adc/ad9361.h
+++ b/linux/drivers/iio/adc/ad9361.h
@@ -180,6 +180,7 @@
 			   enum dig_tune_flags flags);
 int ad9361_tx_mute(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy, u32 state);
 int ad9361_tx_lo_powerdown(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy, bool powerdown);
+bool ad9361_tx_is_muted(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy);
 int ad9361_write_bist_reg(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy, u32 val);
 bool ad9361_uses_rx2tx2(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy);
 bool ad9361_axi_half_dac_rate(struct ad9361_rf_phy *phy);
--- a/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c
+++ b/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c
@@ -1121,7 +1121,25 @@
 		ad9361_tx_lo_powerdown(phy, true);
 	} else {
 		ad9361_tx_lo_powerdown(phy, false);
-		if (ad9361_tx_mute(phy, 0))
+		/*
+		 * Restore the cached attenuation ONLY if nobody has set one
+		 * since we muted.
+		 *
+		 * Setting a gain and then starting the stream is the obvious
+		 * order, and it is the order most tools use. Restoring
+		 * unconditionally overwrites that gain a moment later with a
+		 * value cached at the end of the PREVIOUS transmission - so
+		 * asking for -10 dB could put -60 dB on the wire, with nothing
+		 * to say why. The usual workarounds are to re-apply the gain
+		 * after the stream has started, or a watchdog script polling
+		 * buffer/enable to write a gain of its own; both are symptoms
+		 * of this.
+		 *
+		 * Checking first keeps the convenience - start a stream having
+		 * set nothing, and your last gain comes back - without the
+		 * surprise.
+		 */
+		if (ad9361_tx_is_muted(phy) && ad9361_tx_mute(phy, 0))
 			return;
 	}
 
